Day 69 - Implement Stack using Queues

Problem Statement:
Implement a Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) stack using only two queues.
The stack should support push, pop, top, and empty operations.

Code:
class MyStack {
public:
    queue<int> q1;
    queue<int> q2;

    MyStack() {
        
    }
    
    void push(int x) {
        while(!q1.empty()) {
            q2.push(q1.front());
            q1.pop();
        }

        q1.push(x);

        while(!q2.empty()) {
            q1.push(q2.front());
            q2.pop();
        }
    }
    
    int pop() {
        int ans = q1.front();
        q1.pop();
        return ans;
    }
    
    int top() {
        return q1.front();
    }
    
    bool empty() {
        return q1.empty();
    }
};





Time Complexity:
push() → O(n)
pop() → O(1)
top() → O(1)
empty() → O(1)

Space Complexity:
O(n)

The queues store all n elements.

Approach:
This solution uses two queues and keeps the newest element at the front of q1, so that it behaves like a stack.

For push(x):
Move all elements from q1 to q2.
Push the new element x into q1.
Move all elements from q2 back to q1.
Now, x is at the front of q1.

For pop():
Remove and return the front element of q1.

For top():
Return the front element of q1 without removing it.

For empty():
Check whether q1 is empty.

